-- title:
--   A valid prefix count fits the sparse-query word envelope
-- statement:
--   Let $A,B,C$ be finite index sets with integer values $a,b,c$, and fix integer modulus $p$ and shift $s$. Let $w$ be a wanted item whose source lies in $A$ and whose lifted residue agrees with its global encoding key. Take local parameters $h,J$, depth $k\le J$, and a valid prefix of length $k$: each prefix subset has size $h$ and belongs to the corresponding output block.
--
--   Put $v=(J-k)512h$ and $\rho=(J-k)2h$. For any natural query cutoff $t$, assume the machine word bound $W$ satisfies
--   $$\left(1+\sum_{i=0}^{t}\binom\rho i\right)2^{3v}|B|\,|C|\le W.$$
--   Then the actual raw-incidence prefix count satisfies
--   $$\operatorname{PrefixCount}(w,k,\mathrm{prefix})\le W.$$
--
--   This transfers the sparse-query numerical envelope to the semantic prefix count used by the reporting host.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/c0ldzy17/three-sum-lean/blob/2b639655e07421b9ac743de9090459bab473a7c0/Submission/ThreeSum/ReportingHostSuffixValueBounds.lean#L38-L73

